1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,449
A server is only useful if it has room to work. When a disk fills up, things stop.

2
00:00:05,999 --> 00:00:07,649
A disk holds a filesystem.

3
00:00:07,649 --> 00:00:09,933
A filesystem is mounted on a folder,

4
00:00:09,933 --> 00:00:11,392
called the mount point.

5
00:00:11,392 --> 00:00:15,706
That is how a disk becomes part of the folder tree you already know.

6
00:00:16,256 --> 00:00:17,794
Today you check free space,

7
00:00:17,794 --> 00:00:19,674
format and mount a practice disk,

8
00:00:19,674 --> 00:00:20,927
fill it up on purpose,

9
00:00:20,927 --> 00:00:21,611
and grow it.

10
00:00:23,661 --> 00:00:24,209
First,

11
00:00:24,209 --> 00:00:26,492
how full is this machine?

12
00:00:26,492 --> 00:00:28,228
Type: df -hT /home.

13
00:00:28,228 --> 00:00:30,055
D F means disk free.

14
00:00:30,055 --> 00:00:32,338
Dash h is human readable.

15
00:00:32,338 --> 00:00:35,992
Dash capital T adds the filesystem type.

16
00:00:36,542 --> 00:00:38,461
Size is the whole filesystem.

17
00:00:38,461 --> 00:00:42,034
Used and available are what is taken and what is left.

18
00:00:42,034 --> 00:00:44,350
The last column is the mount point.

19
00:00:44,900 --> 00:00:49,550
Watch the use percent. Above ninety, plan ahead. At one hundred, writes fail.

20
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:50,864
Now,

21
00:00:50,864 --> 00:00:53,046
what is using the space?

22
00:00:53,046 --> 00:00:55,227
Type: du -sh /usr/share.

23
00:00:55,227 --> 00:00:57,137
D U means disk usage.

24
00:00:57,137 --> 00:01:00,591
Dash s gives one total for the folder.

25
00:01:01,141 --> 00:01:02,815
That is the whole folder,

26
00:01:02,815 --> 00:01:03,819
in human units.

27
00:01:03,819 --> 00:01:05,560
It can take a few seconds,

28
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:07,434
because du reads every file.

29
00:01:12,984 --> 00:01:27,534
To find the biggest folders, sort them. Type: du -sh /usr/* | sort -h | tail -n 3.

30
00:01:31,084 --> 00:01:34,924
The biggest folders come last. When space runs out, start with them.

31
00:01:36,974 --> 00:01:39,596
Never practise on a real disk.

32
00:01:39,596 --> 00:01:41,780
So we make a pretend one,

33
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:43,266
an ordinary file.

34
00:01:43,266 --> 00:01:46,062
Type: truncate -s 100M disk.img.

35
00:01:46,612 --> 00:01:50,004
Check it. Type: ls -lh disk.img.

36
00:01:50,554 --> 00:01:53,023
One hundred megabytes of empty space.

37
00:01:53,023 --> 00:01:53,624
To Linux,

38
00:01:53,624 --> 00:01:56,360
a disk is just somewhere to store blocks,

39
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:58,362
and a file can play that part.

40
00:01:59,312 --> 00:02:01,959
Now give it a filesystem.

41
00:02:01,959 --> 00:02:04,923
Type: mkfs.ext4 -q disk.img.

42
00:02:04,923 --> 00:02:07,781
Mkfs means make filesystem,

43
00:02:07,781 --> 00:02:10,533
and dash q keeps it quiet.

44
00:02:11,083 --> 00:02:12,477
No news is good news.

45
00:02:12,477 --> 00:02:14,666
Formatting erases what was there,

46
00:02:14,666 --> 00:02:15,860
so on a real disk,

47
00:02:15,860 --> 00:02:17,718
check the device name twice.

48
00:02:18,668 --> 00:02:24,855
Read the label. Type: sudo blkid -s TYPE -s UUID disk.img.

49
00:02:25,405 --> 00:02:27,227
The type is E X T four,

50
00:02:27,227 --> 00:02:29,921
and the U U I D is its unique I D.

51
00:02:29,921 --> 00:02:32,298
Real systems mount by U U I D,

52
00:02:32,298 --> 00:02:34,834
because device names can change.

53
00:02:36,884 --> 00:02:40,475
A filesystem is not usable until it is mounted.

54
00:02:40,475 --> 00:02:40,934
First,

55
00:02:40,934 --> 00:02:43,149
make a folder to mount it on.

56
00:02:43,149 --> 00:02:44,372
Type: mkdir mnt.

57
00:02:44,922 --> 00:02:45,935
Now mount it.

58
00:02:45,935 --> 00:02:48,274
Type: sudo mount disk.img mnt.

59
00:02:48,274 --> 00:02:49,911
Because it is a file,

60
00:02:49,911 --> 00:02:53,263
Linux attaches it to a loop device for you.

61
00:02:53,813 --> 00:02:56,629
Check it. Type: df -h mnt.

62
00:02:57,179 --> 00:02:58,412
A new filesystem,

63
00:02:58,412 --> 00:02:59,499
mounted on mnt.

64
00:02:59,499 --> 00:03:01,167
About ninety megabytes,

65
00:03:01,167 --> 00:03:04,429
because the filesystem keeps some for itself.

66
00:03:04,429 --> 00:03:06,097
Used is almost nothing.

67
00:03:07,147 --> 00:03:14,571
Now list the block devices. Type: lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,MOUNTPOINTS | grep loop.

68
00:03:15,121 --> 00:03:16,854
Loop zero is the device,

69
00:03:16,854 --> 00:03:18,442
one hundred megabytes,

70
00:03:18,442 --> 00:03:20,103
mounted on your folder.

71
00:03:20,103 --> 00:03:22,052
A real disk looks the same,

72
00:03:22,052 --> 00:03:24,507
with a name like S D A or N V M E.

73
00:03:26,557 --> 00:03:29,427
Now see what a full disk does.

74
00:03:29,427 --> 00:03:32,297
Reserve a sixty megabyte file.

75
00:03:32,297 --> 00:03:35,645
Type: fallocate -l 60M mnt/big.bin.

76
00:03:36,195 --> 00:03:39,672
Check the space. Type: df -h mnt.

77
00:03:40,222 --> 00:03:43,636
Seventy-three percent used, and twenty-three megabytes left.

78
00:03:44,586 --> 00:03:52,138
Now ask for forty more, which will not fit. Type: fallocate -l 40M mnt/more.bin.

79
00:03:52,688 --> 00:03:54,294
No space left on device.

80
00:03:54,294 --> 00:03:58,445
That is what every program sees on a full disk: failed writes,

81
00:03:58,445 --> 00:03:59,248
failed logs,

82
00:03:59,248 --> 00:04:00,453
crashed databases.

83
00:04:01,503 --> 00:04:07,796
Free the space. Type: rm mnt/big.bin mnt/more.bin.

84
00:04:08,346 --> 00:04:11,717
And check again. Type: df -h mnt.

85
00:04:12,267 --> 00:04:17,750
The space is back. Deleting is the fix, but first find what is big, with D U, as you saw.

86
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:21,376
Disks get too small.

87
00:04:21,376 --> 00:04:24,844
Growing has two parts: make the disk bigger,

88
00:04:24,844 --> 00:04:27,367
then make the filesystem bigger.

89
00:04:27,367 --> 00:04:27,840
First,

90
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:28,864
the size now.

91
00:04:28,864 --> 00:04:30,125
Type: df -h mnt.

92
00:04:30,675 --> 00:04:37,118
About ninety megabytes. Unmount it, so the filesystem is quiet. Type: sudo umount mnt.

93
00:04:37,968 --> 00:04:39,626
Make the disk bigger.

94
00:04:39,626 --> 00:04:42,151
Type: truncate -s 200M disk.img.

95
00:04:42,151 --> 00:04:43,572
On a cloud server,

96
00:04:43,572 --> 00:04:47,440
this is where you grow the volume in the console.

97
00:04:47,990 --> 00:04:52,576
Check the filesystem before you resize it.

98
00:04:52,576 --> 00:04:57,817
Type: sudo e2fsck -fy disk.img 2>&1 | tail -n 1.

99
00:04:57,817 --> 00:05:01,421
The 2>&1 part joins the messages,

100
00:05:01,421 --> 00:05:04,587
and tail keeps the last line.

101
00:05:05,137 --> 00:05:08,108
Clean means no problems found.

102
00:05:08,108 --> 00:05:12,167
Now grow the filesystem to fill the disk.

103
00:05:12,167 --> 00:05:16,721
Type: sudo resize2fs disk.img 2>&1 | grep now.

104
00:05:17,671 --> 00:05:23,559
It now fills the bigger disk. Mount it again. Type: sudo mount disk.img mnt.

105
00:05:24,109 --> 00:05:27,160
And check. Type: df -h mnt.

106
00:05:27,710 --> 00:05:30,401
About one hundred eighty-four megabytes.

107
00:05:30,401 --> 00:05:31,074
Same disk,

108
00:05:31,074 --> 00:05:31,747
more room,

109
00:05:31,747 --> 00:05:32,622
nothing lost.

110
00:05:32,622 --> 00:05:33,765
On a real server,

111
00:05:33,765 --> 00:05:36,389
you grow the partition or volume first,

112
00:05:36,389 --> 00:05:38,206
then resize the filesystem.

113
00:05:40,256 --> 00:05:43,570
A mount you make by hand is gone after a reboot.

114
00:05:43,570 --> 00:05:44,330
To keep it,

115
00:05:44,330 --> 00:05:47,851
add one line to the file slash E T C slash F S tab.

116
00:05:48,401 --> 00:05:50,837
Each line has six fields: what to mount,

117
00:05:50,837 --> 00:05:51,202
where,

118
00:05:51,202 --> 00:05:52,420
the filesystem type,

119
00:05:52,420 --> 00:05:52,907
options,

120
00:05:52,907 --> 00:05:55,526
and two numbers for backup and check order.

121
00:05:56,076 --> 00:06:01,900
Use the U U I D from blkid, not the device name, because device names can change.

122
00:06:02,450 --> 00:06:05,119
A wrong line can stop a server booting.

123
00:06:05,119 --> 00:06:06,282
So after editing,

124
00:06:06,282 --> 00:06:07,788
run sudo mount dash a,

125
00:06:07,788 --> 00:06:10,252
and fix any error before you reboot.

126
00:06:10,252 --> 00:06:14,631
The option nofail lets the boot continue if the disk is missing.

127
00:06:16,681 --> 00:06:18,004
Four myths to clear up.

128
00:06:18,554 --> 00:06:22,093
Myth one: delete a file and the space always returns.

129
00:06:22,093 --> 00:06:22,293
No.

130
00:06:22,293 --> 00:06:24,497
If a program still holds it open,

131
00:06:24,497 --> 00:06:27,770
the space stays used until the program closes it.

132
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:30,770
Myth two: formatting is safe to try.

133
00:06:30,770 --> 00:06:30,975
No.

134
00:06:30,975 --> 00:06:32,268
It erases the disk,

135
00:06:32,268 --> 00:06:34,378
so check the device name first.

136
00:06:34,928 --> 00:06:38,306
Myth three: a bigger disk means a bigger filesystem.

137
00:06:38,306 --> 00:06:38,500
No.

138
00:06:38,500 --> 00:06:40,774
You must resize the filesystem too.

139
00:06:41,324 --> 00:06:46,742
Myth four: if it mounted, it will mount at boot. No. Only the F S tab file does that.

140
00:06:47,292 --> 00:06:49,618
Check, mount, grow, then make it permanent.

141
00:06:51,668 --> 00:06:53,993
Here is how you check your own understanding.

142
00:06:54,543 --> 00:06:58,021
Check free space with D F, and say what the use percent means.

143
00:06:58,571 --> 00:07:01,301
Find the biggest folders with D U, and sort them.

144
00:07:01,851 --> 00:07:05,435
Make a filesystem on a practice disk, and read its U U I D.

145
00:07:05,985 --> 00:07:09,249
Mount it, and prove it with D F and L S B L K.

146
00:07:09,799 --> 00:07:12,103
Fill it, read the error, and free the space.

147
00:07:12,653 --> 00:07:17,368
Grow it: bigger disk, check, resize, mount. And say what goes in F S tab.

148
00:07:17,918 --> 00:07:19,539
To pass, you need all six.

149
00:07:21,589 --> 00:07:22,222
Your turn,

150
00:07:22,222 --> 00:07:23,741
in your virtual machine.

151
00:07:23,741 --> 00:07:26,082
Everything here uses a practice file,

152
00:07:26,082 --> 00:07:27,221
never a real disk.

153
00:07:27,771 --> 00:07:31,888
One: run D F dash h T and D U, and name your fullest mount.

154
00:07:32,438 --> 00:07:36,259
Two: make a one hundred megabyte image with truncate,

155
00:07:36,259 --> 00:07:39,286
and format it with M K F S dot E X T four.

156
00:07:39,836 --> 00:07:44,615
Three: read its U U I D with block I D, then mount it and prove it with D F.

157
00:07:45,165 --> 00:07:48,877
Four: fill it with F allocate, read the error, and free the space.

158
00:07:49,427 --> 00:07:54,590
Five: unmount it, grow it to two hundred megabytes, check, resize, and mount it again.

159
00:07:55,140 --> 00:07:59,470
Six: write down the F S tab line you would add, using the U U I D.

160
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:00,344
Last,

161
00:08:00,344 --> 00:08:00,927
clean up.

162
00:08:00,927 --> 00:08:01,446
Unmount,

163
00:08:01,446 --> 00:08:02,871
then delete the image.

164
00:08:02,871 --> 00:08:05,010
Then mark yourself on the rubric.

165
00:08:05,010 --> 00:08:06,047
Pause the video,

166
00:08:06,047 --> 00:08:06,954
and do it now.

167
00:08:22,504 --> 00:08:23,379
Quick check.

168
00:08:23,929 --> 00:08:26,425
Which command shows free space on a filesystem?

169
00:08:26,975 --> 00:08:29,321
Which command finds what is using the space?

170
00:08:29,871 --> 00:08:31,813
Which two steps grow a filesystem?

171
00:08:32,363 --> 00:08:34,197
Which file makes a mount permanent?

172
00:08:34,747 --> 00:08:41,275
Objective covered: MC04-O08.

173
00:08:41,825 --> 00:08:43,895
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

174
00:08:54,445 --> 00:08:57,239
Next lesson: Linux networking and security.

175
00:08:57,789 --> 00:09:01,117
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
